The '''provinces of Sinchew''' are the first-level administrative divisions of the country. Sixteen were constituted in 1736 under the [[Constitution of Sinchew]] of 1733, and the number has changed with the annexation of [[Palpoa Province|Palpoa]].

Each province has an assembly of its own and elects two members of the [[Senate of Sinchew]] under the arrangement in force since 1736. The provinces of the west and centre hold about 31 per cent of the national territory and elect twenty-two of the thirty-two senators; the five provinces of the former [[Hachinawa]] hold about 69 per cent of the territory and elect ten.

== Original Provinces (1736–1828) ==

The sixteen original provinces were constituted in 1736, three years after the constitution that provided for them.

The boundaries were drawn by the Tianist minority in the constituent assembly. The class held about six per cent of the population in 1725 and most of the colony's capital, and it drew small provinces in the west and centre and large ones in the former Hachinawa. The eleven provinces of the west and centre average about 57,700 km²; the five of the former empire average about 278,000 km².

The disproportion was created by two provisions acting together: the equal representation of provinces in the Senate regardless of population, and the election of senators by the provincial assemblies rather than by the population at large. The first made a small province equal to a large one. The second gave the class that dominated the small provinces' electorates the ability to carry their assemblies, and through them the Senate.

The provinces of the former Hachinawa are large because much of their territory was not under the republic's administration when the boundaries were drawn. The largest, [[Hashawa]], contains chiefdoms that had never been subject to the colony, and it was constituted as a single province rather than divided because its settled districts were few.

Each province bears a name in both of the Union's written languages. Names are rendered phonetically in the script that receives them and ordinary words semantically, so the two columns below are not translations of one another.

